WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.890
- Leave?

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:02.723
No, no, no.

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:05.445
Stay right where are.

00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:06.778
Hmm.

00:00:06.778 --> 00:00:10.028
(light mystical music)

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.950
- It's probably the hardest
thing that I've ever worked on.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.930
I like to think of Dark Crystal
as sort of a work of art.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.540
And of all projects, this is the one that

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.663
I'm the most proud of.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:53.212
- I just fell in love with it

00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:54.870
and I've been in love with it ever since.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.860
- I just remember it being quite
an overwhelming experience.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:58.870
Another one of those movies where you

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.800
come out of the cinema slightly changed.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.180
- I remember it vividly.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.460
I loved it.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.424
It's such an incredibly
all encompassing world,

00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:10.243
and the Skeksis are bloody scary.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.390
- People were telling
stories with puppetry

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.020
long before they decided
to come up and act,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.440
because they were too frightened to act

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.750
because they were trying to represent

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.000
all the ancient gods and all the myths.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.360
We're just bringing back,
with the current technologies,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.480
the sort of mythology,
that we've got this desire

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.000
to explore these different storylines

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.730
but we're just being able
to do it through Netflix

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.347
and through animatronics.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.180
- The great thing about voice work

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.020
is that it's inherently
a collaborative process.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.860
And you are far from the most important

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.693
cog in the machine.

00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:56.880
- They have made choices in
movement, in pace, in style.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.650
They've made characterization
choices that you inherit.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.890
- It's a very interesting process

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.490
and it's been very, very
challenging in a good way,

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.820
because usually when you
do a voice in something,

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.720
if you do animation, you do the voice

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.250
and then the animators take your voice

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.030
and they animate to the voice.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.330
With this it's the other way round.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.040
You have to match your
performance to the puppeteers,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.560
who are all very good at what they do.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.400
They are actors, they characterize.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.860
But you have to match their mouth flaps.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.830
- She told you to let go.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.753
She told you to let go.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.760
- To the crystal chamber!

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.790
Ah, scroll keeper!

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:34.623
Scroll keeper!

00:02:34.623 --> 00:02:37.010
You take a lot of time to get it right,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.720
because you can be satisfied that you

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.460
match the movement in sync,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.330
but you try to give performances that are

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.640
as real and as engaging as possible.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.610
- I don't think, puppeteers
aren't well known,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.834
by nature of their job
that they are hidden,

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:52.670
and I don't think, there's
very few famous puppeteers,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.870
it is an art where you choose to hide.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.141
And I very much think
it's like a musician,

00:02:58.141 --> 00:03:00.740
that you have an instrument but you play

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.070
a beautiful tune on it but
we all listen to the tune,

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.190
it's the tune we remember.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.730
It's not necessarily the
piano it was played on,

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.123
it's the character that
lives and has its own life.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.370
- Do you have a plan?

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.203
- Yes.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.140
But you're not gonna like it.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.750
- It's a team ef, like it's
genuinely a team effort.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.920
- It's a huge variety
of people in this film

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.630
from different countries and cultures,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.530
and I think you'll get a sense of that,

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.743
you'll get a sense of
a big eclectic world.

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:42.870
- [Male] All Thra is at risk.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.000
The darkening corrupts
everything it touches.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.210
- What is the darkening?

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.941
- [Male] Behold.

00:03:51.941 --> 00:03:54.691
(dramatic music)

00:04:03.341 --> 00:04:06.640
- Seven clans which are each
run by their own maudra,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.560
who's the matriarch, and
then all the maudras,

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.380
the queen of all of
them is the all-maudra,

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.660
and she's of the Vapra clan.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.820
- One of the things that I
love about fantasy worlds

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.820
is the breakdown of our
own world segregations.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.863
- Remember, the bonds of
sisterhood can be tested.

00:04:30.614 --> 00:04:31.977
But never broken.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.630
- Teddy Biaselli had told me that Henson

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.730
were interested in
doing an animated series

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.740
of The Dark Crystal and I thought about it

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.290
and I went home and I
watched the movie again,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:50.123
and I kept thinking about it.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:52.700
It was like well the mythology is great,

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.390
but what makes it really
special and transcendent

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:56.253
is the puppets.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.260
- I picked up the phone, I was like, okay,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.524
good news bad news.

00:05:01.524 --> 00:05:03.070
Bad news is we're not gonna make

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.330
The Dark Crystal animated series.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.880
The good news is Cindy wants to know

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.110
what it would cost to do it live action.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:12.180
And you could hear the
phone hit the floor.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.360
- And we were shocked.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.790
Like really?

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.160
A series that looks like the film?

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.660
And of course we immediately
responded that it's yes,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.160
like it's technically possible,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.710
but it would be very expensive.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.660
- The best version of The Dark Crystal

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.510
that could be made today would be made by

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.390
the Henson Company, by
giving them the freedom

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:35.400
to really reach for the best version

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.963
they can possibly imagine.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.180
- We as a company have been
doing puppets for over 50 years,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.410
and to be here at this
point, so many years later,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.600
and be doing what is got to be

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.843
the biggest puppet production ever.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:58.163
This has no humans, so it is a
complete puppet extravaganza.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.722
- The best idea is that you watch the show

00:06:05.722 --> 00:06:08.020
and you forget you're watching puppets.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.460
- I think it's wonderful
to revisit something

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.610
that Jim did 35 years ago.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:15.330
The thread that keeps
it all going was Louis,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.190
and I also like to think the
fact that it is Dark Crystal,

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.190
the spiritual thread comes right from Jim,

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.143
all the way through, and Brian as well.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.950
- There's so few people
that know how we did it

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:32.470
the first time, and we're so old

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.303
we don't remember how we
did it the first time.

00:06:35.303 --> 00:06:38.020
- I think it's amazing
that the whole Froud family

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.070
has kinda come back.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.080
Like to see the three of them

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.130
working together is incredible,

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:47.767
and to see him watch his son

00:06:47.767 --> 00:06:50.380
follow in sort of the family business,

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:51.490
but it's not even the family business,

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:52.810
it's like the family world.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.610
- That's where I think
we're sort of thriving

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:59.290
in this fact, is the fact
that we're in this world

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:02.270
that's so natural to us as a family,

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:06.410
that we're able to just
impart what we know

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.960
or what we actually just know to be real.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.490
- I went into the creature workshop

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.400
and it's still the Frouds who are creating

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.200
these wonderful other worldly creations.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.100
And it's Brian Froud's son who was doing

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.670
a lot of the work in there.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.960
And I was like a fan boy meeting him,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.130
'cause he's the baby from Labyrinth.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.560
And then the duality of not only seeing

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.710
someone from a film
that I loved as a child,

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.080
but then also seeing his creative prowess

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.030
and this incredible work he's doing.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.930
It was just fantastic to go
and get a glimpse of all that.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.172
- I like the darkness that's embraced

00:07:40.172 --> 00:07:41.580
with The Dark Crystal.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:42.413
I really do.

00:07:42.413 --> 00:07:46.390
I think sometimes we can
sanitize and spoon feed

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.250
children and young adults a bit too much.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.430
- We have a real respect
for the younger audience,

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.250
which is like you don't
have to shield them

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:54.490
from everything uncomfortable.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:56.870
You actually have to let people experience

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.380
some of like these grittier aspects

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.010
of the world that we live in,

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.500
and maybe the world becomes a little less

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.010
scary because of that.

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:09.435
(upbeat music)

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.360
- What fans of The Dark Crystal want

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.620
is they want an odyssey, they want a saga,

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.760
they want something to enrich the universe

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.240
of the original film, and
that's what this series does.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.820
You can put them side by
side and it is the same world

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.370
but there's just so much
more of it to explore now.

00:08:46.469 --> 00:08:47.636
- Tell no one.

00:08:49.555 --> 00:08:52.388
(dramatic music)

